# apps/web environment. Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) before `pnpm --filter @sam-group/web dev`
# or `build`.
#
# THIS FILE IS NO LONGER OPTIONAL. Before locale routing shipped, `apps/web` read
# no environment at all and a fresh clone built and rendered with no `.env`. That
# is no longer true: the canonical `[locale]` routes are generated from the live
# Locale API, so a build without the variable below fails. See "REQUIRED FOR ANY
# BUILD" further down — it is the most important note in this file.

# --- API ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The NestJS origin, server-side only.
#
# NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, and that is a decision rather than an omission: apps/api
# boots with `cors: false` because API_CONTRACT_FINAL.md §1 states that no
# browser-originated request ever reaches it. Every read is issued from a Server
# Component or from middleware. Prefixing this would inline an internal origin
# into every browser bundle and advertise a surface the architecture says does
# not exist.
#
# ORIGIN ONLY — no path. The `/api/v1` prefix is a frozen architectural constant
# (apps/api `main.ts` sets it globally) and lives in src/lib/api-client.ts, so a
# mistyped .env cannot move the whole API surface.
#
# In development the applications run on the HOST via `pnpm dev`, so this points
# straight at the API's own port. It must match API_PORT in apps/api/.env
# (3001). Going through the local nginx proxy instead would add a hop that can
# fail independently of the thing being served; nginx is the production edge.
#
# In production, web and api are containers behind one nginx (ADR-005) and this
# becomes the compose service name — `http://api:3000`, matching API_UPSTREAM in
# the repository-root .env.
#
# --- REQUIRED FOR ANY BUILD ---------------------------------------------------
#
# `GET /api/v1/locales` is the SOLE routing locale source. `generateStaticParams`
# for the `[locale]` segment reads it to decide which routes exist at all, and
# `<html lang dir>` comes from the same records. The locale list is data in a
# `Locale` table and adding a language must never require a code change
# (PROJECT_HANDOFF.md §6.9), so nothing in the routing layer holds a locale
# literal.
#
# THERE IS NO FALLBACK, BY DECISION. Not to `en`/`fa`/`ar` literals, not to an
# empty array, and not to a static fixture. There WAS one — `LOCALES` in
# features/site/site-routes.ts, which fed only the language switcher and did not
# even share the endpoint's field names — and it was DELETED when that switcher
# started navigating, because a switcher that offers a locale the `Locale` table
# does not have emits a URL the route tree answers with a 404. The active set now
# reaches the switcher from this endpoint, and nothing replaced the fixture. A
# fallback here would not degrade a page; it would generate a DIFFERENT SITE,
# silently, from a build that reported success. A single-locale site or a site
# with zero pages are both worse outcomes than a red build.
#
# So `next build` FAILS, loudly and on purpose, when any of these is true:
#
#   - API_INTERNAL_URL is unset, empty, or not a http(s) origin
#   - the locale endpoint is unreachable, refused, or times out
#   - it answers non-2xx, or with a body that is not the { data, meta } envelope
#   - the active locale set is empty
#   - a row is not { code, name, nativeName, direction, isDefault }
#   - two rows share a `code`
#   - no row, or more than one row, carries `isDefault`
#
# This is a permanent property, not temporary strictness. Set the variable in CI
# and in any environment that builds, and have the API and its database reachable
# from there.
#
# --- WHAT STILL FAILS OPEN, AND WHY THAT IS DIFFERENT -------------------------
#
# The Product Family pages' Category fetch is UNCHANGED and still fails open: if
# the Category API is unavailable, errors, or 404s a registered family, the page
# renders from its fixture and logs the reason (ADR-010 §7). That remains correct
# where it is approved, because the fixture holds the approved content — the only
# API-owned rendered value is the family's name, and the fixture's name is the
# value the database was seeded FROM.
#
# The distinction is the whole point and is worth stating plainly:
#
#   CATEGORY / family CONTENT ....... may fail open to a fixture (approved)
#   LOCALE bootstrap / route generation ... NEVER fails open; it fails the build
#
# A fixture can stand in for what a page says. Nothing can stand in for the list
# of which pages exist.
#
# One consequence at build time worth knowing about, unchanged from before: with
# the variable absent the API client returns "unreachable" WITHOUT issuing a
# request. That used to mean the six proof Category routes were prerendered from
# their fixtures; now the build stops before reaching them, at locale generation.
#
# --- AT RUNTIME ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Middleware reads this variable at request time (it is not inlined into the
# middleware bundle), and only for a REDIRECT CANDIDATE — `/` and the known
# locale-less canonical structural paths. General traffic issues no locale
# request. If locale resolution fails there, the middleware does NOT invent a
# redirect: it logs, passes the request through unchanged, and normal routing
# answers it.
API_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3001

# --- SEO ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both variables below are OPTIONAL and both default to the safe value, so a
# fresh clone behaves exactly as it did before they existed.

# The public origin every absolute URL is built from: canonical tags, hreflang
# alternates, Open Graph URLs, JSON-LD `@id` values, robots.txt and sitemap.xml.
#
# Defaults to https://samgp.com, the frozen public domain (CLAUDE.md §3), which
# is the ONLY origin literal left in apps/web — it lives in
# src/features/seo/site.ts and nowhere else. It used to be copied into eleven
# files, which is eleven places for a canonical, a sitemap entry and a
# structured-data identifier to disagree.
#
# Set it when a build must produce correct absolute URLs for some other origin.
# An unset, empty, malformed or non-http(s) value falls back to the frozen
# domain rather than failing: a canonical URL is not worth failing a build over.
#
# NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_` — every consumer is server-side, and the origin is already
# visible in the page's own canonical tag.
# SITE_PUBLIC_URL=https://samgp.com

# The pre-launch indexing gate. Read by BOTH the `robots` metadata on every
# canonical page and by /robots.txt, so the two cannot contradict each other.
#
# Unset (the default) means `noindex, nofollow` on every page and
# `Disallow: /` in robots.txt — exactly what the platform has served since
# locale routing shipped. P1 is a routing milestone, not the SEO launch.
#
# ONLY the exact string `true` opens the site. `1`, `yes` and `TRUE` all leave
# it closed, deliberately: the failure that matters is opening it by accident.
#
# Do not set this to true until the launch checklist is cleared — approved
# product data, published legal pages, and reviewed fa/ar copy. See the SEO
# report for the current blocker list.
# SITE_SEO_INDEXING=true

# --- Anti-spam (Cloudflare Turnstile) -----------------------------------------
# The PUBLIC site key. Rendered in the browser, so `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is correct and
# required here — unlike API_INTERNAL_URL, this value is meant to be seen. It is
# not a secret; the secret half is TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY in apps/api.
#
# THESE TWO ARE A MATCHING PRODUCTION PAIR, NOT TWO INDEPENDENT SWITCHES.
# A production deployment must set BOTH or the public forms do not work:
#
#   both set        the challenge runs and is verified. The intended production
#                   configuration, and the only one in which a visitor can submit.
#   site key only   a widget nobody verifies. The API has no secret, so in
#                   production it answers 503 and stores nothing.
#   secret only     no token is ever produced. The web client detects this as a
#                   misconfiguration and BLOCKS both forms before anything is
#                   typed; had it not, the API would refuse every submission 403.
#   neither set     in PRODUCTION: both forms are blocked, with a notice saying
#                   the security check could not be completed, and the API
#                   answers 503. Nothing is submitted and nothing is stored.
#                   OUTSIDE PRODUCTION: the development no-op — no widget, no
#                   script, nothing blocked, rate limiting alone.
#
# There is deliberately no variable that switches the challenge off in a
# production build. The only way to have working public forms in production is to
# configure both halves.
#
# The site key must be created in a mode that does not present a challenge to
# ordinary visitors — SITE_STRUCTURE.md §10 requires an INVISIBLE captcha, not a
# visible one. A "Managed" widget combined with the `interaction-only` appearance
# this app sets, or an "Invisible" widget, both satisfy that. Add samgp.com (and
# any preview hostname) to the key's allowed domains.
#
# TWO CONSEQUENCES OF CONFIGURING IT, both deliberate:
#
#   - The submit control on both public forms stays DISABLED until a token has
#     been issued, and JavaScript is required to obtain one. A visitor with
#     JavaScript disabled is told so rather than being allowed to submit and
#     refused.
#   - The control FAILS CLOSED. If Cloudflare cannot be reached, the form says
#     the check could not be completed and nothing is submitted. Lead capture
#     through these two forms stops for the duration.
#
# NOTE: this is the ONLY third-party request any public page makes, and it is
# made only when a site key is set — an unconfigured deployment contacts nobody,
# in either environment. Fonts are self-hosted precisely to avoid one. The
# Privacy Policy must name Cloudflare as a processor before it is published;
# that disclosure is tracked in the Legal workstream.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
